<code>assert index >= 0 && index <= \old(size) => \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < index; (this.get(i) == null && \old(this.get(i)) == null) || this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i))));</code>
--------------------
<code>assert index >= 0 && index <= \old(size) => \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < index; (this.get(i) != null && this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i)))) || (this.get(i) == null && \old(this.get(i)) == null);</code>
--------------------
Let's break down the requirement in the comment:

- The condition is that the `index` must be between `0` and the initial size of the list (`size`) inclusive.
- The assertion is about the elements before the `index`. Specifically, for every valid index `i` less than the specified `index`, the element at `i` remains unchanged or is `null` both before and after the operation.

Given the above, we need to check that for all `i` in the range `[0, index)`, the elements do not change. We can use the `\old` syntax to refer to the state before the operation and `\result` to refer to the state after the operation.

Here is the assert statement that captures this requirement:

<code>assert index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size()) => \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < index; this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i)));</code>

This ensures that if the `index` is within the valid range, all elements before the `index` remain unchanged.
